{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,105p' .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,100p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '1,260p' scripts/build_artifacts.py; cat scripts/check_paper.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,105p' .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; sed -n '1,100p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '1,260p' scripts/build_artifacts.py; cat scripts/check_paper.py\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n# Paper Strategy: Frozen-Score Mean Inference\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status:** ready. **Family:** `paper_standalone`, compatible with `write-paper-standalone`.\n\n**Provisional title:** *Frozen-Score Mean Inference: Width Gains and Coverage Limits*.\n\n**Central question:** With labels, unlabeled scores, and the predictor fixed within each comparison, what interval-width gains and coverage limitations does honest score calibration exhibit for a binary population mean relative to prediction-powered and labeled-only intervals?\n\n**Central thesis:** Across seven evaluated binary-mean conditions, honest score calibration has a small paired width advantage over the evaluated PPI++ comparator in only one condition; rare-outcome interval collapse shows why widths need coverage uncertainty and an exact-binomial reference alongside them.\n\nThis is a standalone empirical/methodological limitations paper, not a claim of universal improvement or a demonstration that nonlinear calibration caused the observed gain. The shared object is binary superpopulation-mean inference from immutable inputs. Present neutral conditions C1-C7 as evidence blocks under that question, not as named application studies or chronological experiments.\n\n## Evidence and Qualification\n\nRead `paper_data.json.final_evaluation` independently. It reports a completed, qualified final evaluation. The authoritative qualification anchors are `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct`; no failed final qualification is reported here. This status is not a guarantee of nominal statistical coverage. Missing gate-specific diagnostics remain unknown, and neither replay flags nor unverified candidate diagnostics prove statistical validity. If later authoritative evidence changes qualification, preserve the reported outcome and limitations through writing, review, audit, and revision without guessing the cause.\n\nThe numerical anchor is `final.evidence.episodes`: 256 seeded repetitions, each containing seven conditions and paired candidate/baseline intervals. The aggregate case summaries occur only at `episodes[0].solution.case_summaries`; individual records are under `episodes[*].solution.cases`. All seven coverage summaries, paired mean width differences, and baseline zero-width counts were checked against those records. The diagnostic-trust annotation is `unverified`; the plan relies on recorded endpoints, truths, and comparisons, not on candidate-supplied validity assertions.\n\nThe final aggregate coverage is **0.9425223214285714** and mean width **0.12988779848794016** (`final.metric.coverage`, `final.metric.mean_width`). These are descriptive, not proof of 95% validity. Do not treat all seven within-seed cases as independent when constructing aggregate uncertainty. Per-condition candidate coverage ranges from **0.9296875** to **0.95703125**; every reported candidate coverage interval includes 0.95. Non-rejection is not equivalence or validation of nominal coverage.\n\n`downstream_study` and `study.*` grounding sources are absent. Omit search-score deltas, scored/unscored attempt accounting, and superiority to an original search incumbent. Missing evaluations are not measured losses. The measured PPI++ comparator is not automatically the original search incumbent. Internal selection metadata is private provenance, not a visible paper result.\n\n## Framing Alternatives and Edit Decision\n\nNo resolved edit-context files, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` are staged. This is fresh framing, not an update to a prior manuscript. The JSON enum is **recentered** only to describe recentering the broad task opportunity around the actual final evidence; it does not assert a prior paper existed.\n\n- **Preserve a generic improvement story:** reject. Only one condition is narrower than evaluated PPI++; finite-sample validity of the returned method is not established.\n- **Broaden to a shared family of searched methods:** reject. Other branches do not supply the necessary measured comparisons; source-only functions and unsuccessful executions are not performance evidence.\n- **Center the evaluated score-control method's width/coverage limits:** select. It has repeated paired measurements, uncertainty, a small isolated gain, widespread modest costs, and an informative low-mean failure comparison.\n\nThe selected candidate source is under `results/ideas/02_honest_nonlinear_score_controls_with_a_finite_sample_safety_track/program_1/src/`, linked by `result.top_program_01`. Inspect `program.py`, `METHOD.md`, and `study.py` directly for implementation facts. The final evidence records a frozen program-tree digest, but a separate final source tree is not staged for file-by-file comparison. The candidate's preparation notes predate execution; final episode records supersede their absence-of-results statements, not their warnings about asymptotic versus safety intervals.\n\n## Method Groups\n\n1. **Honest frozen-score controls — proposed.** Two-fold complementary-label calibration; ridge affine and small piecewise-linear spline candidates selected by training-only GCV; clipped controls; finite-unlabeled-sample factor `rho=N/(n+N)`; residual correction. The evaluated output is a Wilson-style stabilized asymptotic Wald interval, with a size-only Clopper-Pearson branch for fewer than 16 labels. Do not call the general procedure exact or an exact Wilson interval.\n2. **Evaluated PPI++ mean interval — primary baseline.** The paired efficiency comparison is central. Restrict claims to this evaluated comparator rather than all implementations of the published method.\n3. **Evaluated PPI mean interval — baseline.** Retain in the main table, rare-outcome plot, and complete supplement. Its behavior differs materially from PPI++.\n4. **Labeled-only asymptotic mean interval — baseline.** Separates gains over label-only normal inference from gains over prediction-powered inference.\n5. **Labeled-only exact binomial interval — baseline.** Finite-sample reference under iid Bernoulli sampling, not permission to transfer its guarantee to another estimator.\n6. **Bounded componentwise safety construction — supporting theory only.** The source implements empirical-Bernstein component intervals and union-bound/fixed-weight aggregation separately. It was not the measured primary output; do not assign it empirical coverage or width.\n\nExclude partition portfolios and likelihood branches from the main comparison. An affine-only matched calibration function exists but has no measured ablation in the supplied evidence. Likewise, the oracle frontier and safety helper are not executed evidence of superiority.\n\nKeep the target **E[Y]**, not the realized finite-pool mean. Inputs are fixed across methods within each repetition; frequentist uncertainty is over repeated draws. Common-population iid sampling and a predictor frozen independently of inference labels are assumptions, not established facts about unidentified generators. Explain the fixed-control variance decomposition, conditional-mean oracle, affine projection gap, and fold-variance approximation with their separate assumptions. A fixed clipped spline dictionary need not attain the oracle; cross-fitting does not make folds unconditionally independent.\n\n## Results That Carry the Story\n\nUse `final.evidence.episodes` for this entire section. Retain C1-C7 identifiers: the records do not identify datasets, generators, or case-specific sample sizes. Do not infer a nonlinear, shifted, or adversarial regime from a result pattern.\n\n- **One small PPI++ width gain:** in C2, candidate-minus-PPI++ mean width difference is **-0.00030173260155952255**, with reported 95% interval **[-0.0004003584986521721, -0.000203106704466873]**. The other six differences are positive. These are pointwise, condition-specific findings, not multiplicity-adjusted general improvement or evidence attributing the effect to splines.\n- **Label-only context:** C1-C3 are narrower than labeled-only asymptotic inference; C4, C6, and C7 have small width increases against that baseline. Report the exact-binomial and PPI comparisons too, rather than selecting only favorable baselines.\n- **Rare-outcome diagnostic:** C5 records truth **0.006800000000000005**. PPI++ and labeled-only asymptotic coverage are each **0.55078125**, with **115** zero-width intervals each. Candidate coverage is **0.9296875**, with no zero-width intervals; exact-binomial coverage is **0.99609375**. PPI has two zero-width intervals. Collapse and undercoverage are observable; the evaluator's internal cause for every miss is not identified.\n- **Exact reference remains important:** in C5, candidate-minus-exact mean width difference is **-0.000046988429386779555**, with interval **[-0.00032065914173324357, 0.0002266822829596845]**. This width comparison is inconclusive. The candidate's absence of collapsed intervals does not establish nominal validity or superiority to exact inference.\n- **Avoid unstable ratio headlines:** the retained aggregate width ratio is **64175.10539621905**. Zero and near-zero comparator widths make ratio summaries scientifically misleading; foreground paired differences and raw width distributions. Do not infer the evaluator's denominator regularization.\n\nThe main table must include all five evaluated inferential methods. Split coverage and width blocks if necessary. Keep the C2 gain, six PPI++ widenings, C5 collapse counts, and candidate coverage caveat in the main body. The `[0,1]` sanity interval is not a headline efficiency baseline. Do not translate evaluator flags into scientific validity claims.\n\n## Figures and Practical Geometry\n\nInspected `paper.sty` and `.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex`. The style has **one 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height**. There is no two-column body: all required figures use full text width, not `figure*` to span nonexistent columns. The scaffold's author and final-copy mode must be replaced for anonymous review during later drafting. Practical geometry does not certify the official 2026 template version; verify that provenance before submission.\n\n### Figure 1 — `frozen_score_overview`\n\nRequired early conceptual diagram; generator **`generate-concept-figure`**. Place on page 1 or 2, preferably page 2 beside the problem definition. Target **5.5 × 1.8 inches**, full text width, approximately one quarter page including caption. Five grouped objects show immutable inputs, complementary-fold fitting/evaluation, residual correction targeting E[Y], the evaluated stabilized primary interval, and a separate unmeasured safety construction. Use solid versus dashed connectors for evaluated versus theoretical paths, not chronological discovery arrows. No predictor retraining or empirical-gain badge.\n\nReadability budget: **one panel, one panel per row, at most five object groups, three semantic series, labels at least 9 points**. Half-width would crowd fold isolation and track distinctions. Grounding: `result.top_program_01` for the source path, `final.evidence.episodes` for which primary output was measured. Retain editable draw.io output and prompt log; no figure generation occurs in this stage.\n\n### Figure 2 — `coverage_width_comparison`\n\nRequired matplotlib composite; target **5.5 × 4.3 inches**, full text width, approximately three fifths page including caption. Use a **2 × 2 grid**: coverage left, paired width differences right; C1-C4 in the upper row, C5-C7 below. Keep fixed case order, not performance ranking. Each panel has at most four conditions, so seven cases do not crowd one axis.\n\nCoverage series: candidate, evaluated PPI++, labeled-only asymptotic, exact binomial. Show the reported coverage uncertainty and 0.95 reference. Difference series: candidate minus PPI++, labeled-only asymptotic, and exact; show reported uncertainty and zero reference. Negative means narrower, not automatically valid. PPI remains in the main table and rare-case figure. Clearly labeled different linear scales are permissible across width panels because C5 is much larger.\n\nReadability budget: **four panels, two per row, four categories per panel, four series maximum; final labels at least 8.5 points**. Approximately 2.4-inch subpanels, concise case labels, and shared legends make this possible without shrinking. Grounding: `final.evidence.episodes`, particularly the single attached set of case summaries. Explain that the uncertainty is pointwise, not simultaneous.\n\n### Figure 3 — `rare_outcome_diagnostic`\n\nRequired matplotlib diagnostic; target **5.5 × 2.2 inches**, full text width, approximately one third page including caption. Two side-by-side panels show C5 empirical interval-width CDFs and zero-width counts for candidate, evaluated PPI++, evaluated PPI, and exact binomial. Retain the mass at zero on a linear width axis. Mention the labeled-only asymptotic baseline's matching 115 collapses in the caption and main table. Put coverage and its uncertainty in adjacent main text, not only the supplement.\n\nReadability budget: **two panels, two per row, four method categories, four series; final labels at least 8.5 points**. Full width gives each panel enough room for axes and method labels. Grounding: all 256 C5 endpoint records in `final.evidence.episodes`. Do not imply the collapse diagnostic establishes the internal cause of every coverage miss.\n\n### Export Contract\n\nQuantitative plotting source emits **PDF and 300-DPI PNG**, and LaTeX references the PDF whenever available. Preserve editable concept output and export PDF when possible. For truly raster-only figures, regenerate at **at least 2400 pixels wide full-width or 1200 pixels wide column-width**; never upscale an existing raster. Use consistent method colors and validity labels. Exactly one overview uses `generate-concept-figure`; follow its later orchestration handoff rather than rendering it during strategy generation.\n\n## Body Budget and Organization\n\nTarget **8.5 pages excluding references**, with figure footprints included:\n\n| Scientific content | Planned pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Abstract, introduction, concrete contributions | 1.25 |\n| Problem and related work, including early overview | 1.25 |\n| Honest controls and limited theoretical analysis | 2.00 |\n| Evaluation design and provenance limitations | 1.00 |\n| Direct coverage/width comparison and compact table | 1.75 |\n| Rare-outcome analysis | 0.75 |\n| Limitations and conclusion | 0.50 |\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import binomtest, t\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nDATA = ROOT / \"paper_data.json\"\nMETHODS = [\"candidate\", \"ppi_plus_plus_mean\", \"prediction_powered_mean\",\n           \"labeled_only_asymptotic_mean\", \"labeled_only_exact_binomial\"]\nLABELS = [\"Score control\", \"PPI++\", \"PPI\", \"Label normal\", \"Exact binomial\"]\nCOLORS = [\"#007C83\", \"#CB6438\", \"#8E689D\", \"#758195\", \"#31548B\"]\n\n\ndef provenance(path, inputs=None):\n    payload = {\n        \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n        \"artifact\": {\"path\": str(path.relative_to(ROOT)), \"kind\": \"derived\",\n                     \"sha256\": hashlib.sha256(path.read_bytes()).hexdigest()},\n        \"derived_from\": inputs or [\"@evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\"],\n        \"generated_by\": {\"script_path\": \"scripts/build_artifacts.py\",\n                         \"command\": [\"python\", \"scripts/build_artifacts.py\"]},\n    }\n    Path(str(path) + \".prov.json\").write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n\n\ndef save_panel(figure, name):\n    for extension in [\"pdf\", \"png\"]:\n        path = ROOT / \"figures\" / f\"{name}.{extension}\"\n        figure.savefig(path, dpi=300, facecolor=\"white\")\n        provenance(path)\n    plt.close(figure)\n\n\ndef mean_interval(values):\n    values = np.asarray(values)\n    center = float(values.mean())\n    margin = float(t.ppf(.975, len(values) - 1) * values.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(len(values)))\n    return {\"mean\": center, \"lower95\": center - margin, \"upper95\": center + margin}\n\n\ndef main():\n    # @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    data = json.loads(DATA.read_text())\n    episodes = data[\"final_evaluation\"][\"evaluation_evidence\"][\"episodes\"]\n    summaries = episodes[0][\"solution\"][\"case_summaries\"]\n    results = []\n    for position, reference in enumerate(summaries):\n        records = [episode[\"solution\"][\"cases\"][position] for episode in episodes]\n        assert all(record[\"case\"] == reference[\"case\"] for record in records)\n        truth_values = sorted(set(record[\"truth\"] for record in records))\n        row = {\"case\": reference[\"case\"], \"truths\": truth_values, \"methods\": {}, \"differences\": {}}\n        for method in METHODS:\n            intervals = [record[\"response\"] if method == \"candidate\" else record[\"baselines\"][method] for record in records]\n            widths = np.array([item[\"upper\"] - item[\"lower\"] for item in intervals])\n            covered = np.array([item[\"lower\"] <= record[\"truth\"] <= item[\"upper\"] for item, record in zip(intervals, records)])\n            bounds = binomtest(int(covered.sum()), len(covered)).proportion_ci(method=\"exact\")\n            row[\"methods\"][method] = {\"coverage\": float(covered.mean()), \"covered\": int(covered.sum()),\n                \"coverage_lower95\": float(bounds.low), \"coverage_upper95\": float(bounds.high),\n                \"width\": mean_interval(widths), \"zero_width_count\": int(np.count_nonzero(widths == 0)),\n                \"widths\": widths.tolist()}\n        candidate_widths = np.array(row[\"methods\"][\"candidate\"][\"widths\"])\n        for method in METHODS[1:]:\n            row[\"differences\"][method] = mean_interval(candidate_widths - row[\"methods\"][method][\"widths\"])\n            recorded = reference[\"comparisons\"][method][\"difference\"]\n            assert np.isclose(row[\"differences\"][method][\"mean\"], recorded[\"mean_difference\"], atol=1e-12)\n            assert np.isclose(row[\"differences\"][method][\"lower95\"], recorded[\"lower95\"], atol=1e-10)\n        results.append(row)\n    out = {\"repetitions\": len(episodes), \"seeds\": [episode[\"seed\"] for episode in episodes],\n           \"case_count\": len(results), \"cases\": results,\n           \"interpretation\": \"Pointwise exact binomial coverage intervals; paired t width-difference intervals. No multiplicity correction or new evaluation.\"}\n    path = ROOT / \"analysis_results.json\"\n    path.write_text(json.dumps(out, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    provenance(path)\n    headers = \"Condition & Score control & PPI++ & PPI & Label normal & Exact binomial\"\n    coverage_rows = [headers + r\" \\\\\", r\"\\midrule\"]\n    width_rows = [headers + r\" \\\\\", r\"\\midrule\"]\n    for row in results:\n        coverage_rows.append(\"C%d & \" % row[\"case\"] + \" & \".join(f'{row[\"methods\"][method][\"coverage\"]:.3f}' for method in METHODS) + r\" \\\\\")\n        width_rows.append(\"C%d & \" % row[\"case\"] + \" & \".join(f'{row[\"methods\"][method][\"width\"][\"mean\"]:.5f}' for method in METHODS) + r\" \\\\\")\n    for name, rows in [(\"coverage\", coverage_rows), (\"width\", width_rows)]:\n        path = ROOT / \"tables\" / f\"{name}.tex\"\n        path.write_text(\"% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(rows) + \"\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\")\n        provenance(path)\n    extended = []\n    for row in results:\n        for method, label in zip(METHODS, LABELS):\n            value = row[\"methods\"][method]\n            extended.append(f'C{row[\"case\"]} & {label} & {value[\"coverage\"]:.3f} [{value[\"coverage_lower95\"]:.3f}, {value[\"coverage_upper95\"]:.3f}] & {value[\"width\"][\"mean\"]:.5f} & {value[\"zero_width_count\"]}' + r\" \\\\\")\n        extended.append(r\"\\midrule\")\n    path = ROOT / \"tables\" / \"complete.tex\"\n    path.write_text(\"% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(extended) + \"\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\")\n    provenance(path)\n    differences = []\n    for row in results:\n        for method, label in zip(METHODS[1:], LABELS[1:]):\n            value = row[\"differences\"][method]\n            differences.append(f'C{row[\"case\"]} & {label} & {value[\"mean\"]:+.6f} & [{value[\"lower95\"]:+.6f}, {value[\"upper95\"]:+.6f}]' + r\" \\\\\")\n        differences.append(r\"\\midrule\")\n    path = ROOT / \"tables\" / \"differences.tex\"\n    path.write_text(\"% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(differences) + \"\\n\\\\bottomrule\\n\")\n    provenance(path)\n    plt.rcParams.update({\"font.size\": 9, \"axes.titlesize\": 9, \"axes.labelsize\": 9,\n                         \"xtick.labelsize\": 8.5, \"ytick.labelsize\": 9,\n                         \"axes.spines.top\": False, \"axes.spines.right\": False,\n                         \"pdf.fonttype\": 42, \"ps.fonttype\": 42})\n    plotted = [0, 1, 3, 4]\n    figure, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(5.5, 4.3), gridspec_kw={\"width_ratios\": [1, 1.1]})\n    for group_index, indices in enumerate([range(4), range(4, 7)]):\n        coverage_axis, width_axis = axes[group_index]\n        offsets = np.linspace(-.23, .23, len(plotted))\n        for method_index, offset in zip(plotted, offsets):\n            for local_index, case_index in enumerate(indices):\n                value = results[case_index][\"methods\"][METHODS[method_index]]\n                coverage_axis.errorbar(value[\"coverage\"], local_index + offset,\n                    xerr=[[value[\"coverage\"] - value[\"coverage_lower95\"]], [value[\"coverage_upper95\"] - value[\"coverage\"]]],\n                    fmt=\"o\", ms=3.4, lw=.8, color=COLORS[method_index], capsize=1.5)\n        for method_index, offset in zip([1, 3, 4], [-.18, 0, .18]):\n            for local_index, case_index in enumerate(indices):\n                value = results[case_index][\"differences\"][METHODS[method_index]]\n                width_axis.errorbar(1000 * value[\"mean\"], local_index + offset,\n                    xerr=[[1000 * (value[\"mean\"] - value[\"lower95\"])], [1000 * (value[\"upper95\"] - value[\"mean\"])]],\n                    fmt=\"o\", ms=3.4, lw=.8, color=COLORS[method_index], capsize=1.5)\n        for axis in [coverage_axis, width_axis]:\n            axis.set_yticks(range(len(indices)), [f\"C{position + 1}\" for position in indices])\n            axis.set_ylim(len(indices)-.5, -.5)\n            axis.grid(axis=\"x\", alpha=.13)\n        coverage_axis.axvline(.95, ls=\"--\", lw=.8, color=\"#444444\")\n        coverage_axis.set_xlim((.84, 1.005) if group_index == 0 else (.48, 1.015))\n        width_axis.axvline(0, ls=\"--\", lw=.8, color=\"#444444\")\n    axes[0, 0].set_title(\"(a) Coverage: C1–C4\", loc=\"left\")\n    axes[0, 1].set_title(\"(b) Width differences: C1–C4\", loc=\"left\")\n    axes[1, 0].set_title(\"(c) Coverage: C5–C7\", loc=\"left\")\n    axes[1, 1].set_title(\"(d) Width differences: C5–C7\", loc=\"left\")\n    axes[1, 0].set_xlabel(\"Empirical coverage\")\n    axes[1, 1].set_xlabel(\"Width difference (×10⁻³)\")\n    handles = [plt.Line2D([], [], color=COLORS[index], marker=\"o\", ms=4, lw=1, label=LABELS[index]) for index in plotted]\n    figure.legend(handles=handles, loc=\"upper center\", ncol=2, frameon=False, fontsize=9, bbox_to_anchor=(.51, 1.005))\n    figure.subplots_adjust(left=.09, right=.98, bottom=.13, top=.79, wspace=.46, hspace=.62)\n    save_panel(figure, \"coverage_width_comparison\")\n    figure, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(5.5, 2.3), gridspec_kw={\"width_ratios\": [1.25, 1]})\n    rare = results[4]\n    for method_index in [0, 1, 2, 4]:\n        values = np.sort(rare[\"methods\"][METHODS[method_index]][\"widths\"])\n        axes[0].step(np.r_[0, values], np.r_[0, np.arange(1, len(values)+1)/len(values)], where=\"post\", color=COLORS[method_index], lw=1.25, label=LABELS[method_index])\n    axes[0].set(xlabel=\"Interval width\", ylabel=\"Empirical CDF\", ylim=(0, 1.03), xlim=(0, None))\n    axes[0].set_title(\"(a) C5 width distribution\", loc=\"left\")\n    selected = [0, 1, 2, 4]\n    counts = [rare[\"methods\"][METHODS[index]][\"zero_width_count\"] for index in selected]\n    axes[1].barh(range(4), counts, color=[COLORS[index] for index in selected], height=.6)\n    axes[1].set_yticks(range(4), [LABELS[index] for index in selected], fontsize=8.5)\n    axes[1].invert_yaxis()\n    axes[1].set_xlim(0, max(counts)*1.25)\n    axes[1].set_xlabel(\"Zero-width intervals\")\n    for position, count in enumerate(counts):\n        axes[1].text(count + 2, position, str(count), va=\"center\", fontsize=9)\n    axes[1].set_title(\"(b) Collapse counts\", loc=\"left\")\n    handles, labels = axes[0].get_legend_handles_labels()\n    figure.legend(handles, labels, loc=\"upper center\", ncol=4, frameon=False, fontsize=8.5, bbox_to_anchor=(.5, 1.015), handlelength=1.2, columnspacing=.9)\n    figure.subplots_adjust(left=.1, right=.99, bottom=.24, top=.73, wspace=.67)\n    save_panel(figure, \"rare_outcome_diagnostic\")\n    concept = ROOT / \"figures\" / \"frozen_score_overview.png\"\n    prompt_log = concept.with_suffix(\".prompts.json\")\n    if not (ROOT / \".concept_figure_complete\").exists() and not concept.exists():\n        from PIL import Image, ImageDraw\n        image = Image.new(\"RGB\", (2400, 630), \"white\")\n        draw = ImageDraw.Draw(image)\n        draw.text((50, 280), \"Concept figure reserved for synchronous rendering\", fill=\"#666666\")\n        image.save(concept)\n    if not prompt_log.exists():\n        prompt_log.write_text(\"{}\\n\")\n    figures = []\n    for name, panels, categories, series, height in [\n        (\"frozen_score_overview\", 1, 4, 2, 1.8),\n        (\"coverage_width_comparison\", 4, 4, 4, 4.3),\n        (\"rare_outcome_diagnostic\", 2, 4, 4, 2.3)]:\n        entry = {\"figure_id\": name, \"path_png\": f\"figures/{name}.png\",\n                 \"figure_type\": \"diagram\" if panels == 1 else \"composite\",\n                 \"n_panels\": panels, \"panels_per_row\": 1 if panels == 1 else 2,\n                 \"categories_per_panel\": [categories] * panels, \"series_per_panel\": [series] * panels,\n                 \"max_label_chars\": 30, \"font_sizes\": {\"title\": 9, \"axis\": 9, \"legend\": 8.5, \"ticks\": 8.5},\n                 \"uses_abbrev_labels\": True, \"uses_progression\": False,\n                 \"source_ids\": [] if panels == 1 else [\"final.evidence.episodes\"],\n                 \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\" if panels == 1 else \"matplotlib\",\n                 \"placement\": \"full_width\", \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n                 \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5}\n        if panels == 1:\n            entry[\"prompt_log_path\"] = f\"figures/{name}.prompts.json\"\n        else:\n            entry[\"path_pdf\"] = f\"figures/{name}.pdf\"\n        figures.append(entry)\n    (ROOT / \"figure_manifest.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"figures\": figures}, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    for row in results:\n        print(\"C\", row[\"case\"], \"truth\", row[\"truths\"], \"coverage\", row[\"methods\"][\"candidate\"][\"coverage\"], \"width\", row[\"methods\"][\"candidate\"][\"width\"][\"mean\"], \"PPI++ difference\", row[\"differences\"][METHODS[1]])\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\nimport importlib.util\nimport json\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport jsonschema\nimport pymupdf\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\n\ndef main():\n    data = json.loads((ROOT / \"paper_data.json\").read_text())\n    strategy = json.loads((ROOT / \"paper_strategy.json\").read_text())\n    manifest = json.loads((ROOT / \"figure_manifest.json\").read_text())\n    schema = json.loads((ROOT / \".codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\").read_text())\n    jsonschema.validate(manifest, schema)\n    specifications = {item[\"figure_id\"]: item for item in strategy[\"figure_specs\"]}\n    manuscript = (ROOT / \"paper.tex\").read_text()\n    declared_paths = set()\n    for figure in manifest[\"figures\"]:\n        spec = specifications[figure[\"figure_id\"]]\n        assert figure[\"n_panels\"] <= spec[\"max_panels\"]\n        assert max(figure[\"categories_per_panel\"]) <= spec[\"max_categories_per_panel\"]\n        assert max(figure[\"series_per_panel\"]) <= spec[\"max_series_per_panel\"]\n        assert figure[\"panels_per_row\"] <= spec[\"max_panels_per_row\"]\n        assert figure[\"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\"] >= 8\n        assert set(figure[\"source_ids\"]) <= set(data[\"grounding_sources\"])\n        for field in [\"path_png\", \"path_pdf\", \"prompt_log_path\"]:\n            if field in figure:\n                assert (ROOT / figure[field]).is_file()\n                declared_paths.add(figure[field])\n    for included in re.findall(r\"\\\\includegraphics(?:\\[[^\\]]*\\])?\\{([^}]+)\\}\", manuscript):\n        assert included in declared_paths, included\n    assert set(specifications) == {item[\"figure_id\"] for item in manifest[\"figures\"]}\n    resolved_json = 0\n    resolved_lines = 0\n    for token in re.findall(r\"@evidence\\[\\[([^\\]]+)\\]\\]\", manuscript):\n        target, separator, locator = token.partition(\"#\")\n        path = ROOT / target\n        assert path.is_file(), token\n        if path.suffix == \".json\" and locator.startswith(\"/\"):\n            value = json.loads(path.read_text())\n            for part in locator[1:].split(\"/\"):\n                part = part.replace(\"~1\", \"/\").replace(\"~0\", \"~\")\n                value = value[int(part)] if isinstance(value, list) else value[part]\n            resolved_json += 1\n        if re.fullmatch(r\"L\\d+(?:-L?\\d+)?\", locator):\n            numbers = [int(value) for value in re.findall(r\"\\d+\", locator)]\n            assert max(numbers) <= len(path.read_text().splitlines()), token\n            resolved_lines += 1\n    pdf = pymupdf.open(ROOT / \"paper.pdf\")\n    texts = [page.get_text() for page in pdf]\n    reference_index = next(index for index, text in enumerate(texts) if re.search(r\"\\bREFERENCES\\b\", text))\n    supplement_index = next(index for index, text in enumerate(texts) if \"SUPPLEMENTARY INFORMATION\" in text)\n    assert reference_index in [8, 9], reference_index\n    assert supplement_index > reference_index\n    assert \"LLM USAGE\" in \"\\n\".join(texts)\n    forbidden = r\"\\b(?:ideas?|programs?|attempts?|eligible|worker_01|worker_02|combined_score|rubric)\\b\"\n    for page_text in texts:\n        scrubbed = re.sub(r\"program\\.py\", \"source-file\", page_text)\n        assert not re.search(forbidden, scrubbed, re.I), re.search(forbidden, scrubbed, re.I)\n    body_last = pdf[reference_index - 1]\n    rectangles = []\n    for block in body_last.get_text(\"dict\")[\"blocks\"]:\n        for line in block.get(\"lines\", []):\n            spans = [span for span in line[\"spans\"] if span[\"bbox\"][0] >= 106 and span[\"bbox\"][2] <= 506\n                     and 70 <= span[\"bbox\"][1] < 730 and not span[\"text\"].isdigit()]\n            rectangles.extend(span[\"bbox\"] for span in spans)\n    extent = max(rectangle[3] for rectangle in rectangles) - min(rectangle[1] for rectangle in rectangles)\n    fill = extent / 648\n    assert fill >= .75 if reference_index == 8 else True\n    specification = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"rendered_si\", ROOT / \".codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py\")\n    validator = importlib.util.module_from_spec(specification)\n    specification.loader.exec_module(validator)\n    body_texts = [page.get_text(clip=pymupdf.Rect(105, 65, 507, 750), sort=True) for page in pdf]\n    errors = validator.validate_rendered_supplementary_information(body_texts)\n    assert not errors, errors\n    summary = json.loads((ROOT / \"scientific-evidence/verification-report.json\").read_text())[\"summary\"]\n    assert summary[\"dangling_references\"] == 0\n    report = {\"status\": \"local_checks_passed\", \"body_pages\": reference_index, \"total_pages\": len(pdf),\n              \"last_body_page_vertical_extent_fraction\": fill,\n              \"geometry_note\": \"Local text-span extent, excluding margin counters; not the parent orchestrator's authoritative occupancy metric.\",\n              \"figure_schema\": \"passed\", \"strategy_figure_budgets\": \"passed\", \"figures\": len(manifest[\"figures\"]),\n              \"resolved_manuscript_json_pointers\": resolved_json, \"resolved_source_line_locators\": resolved_lines,\n              \"rendered_supplement\": \"passed canonical heading/content function on PyMuPDF text within the official text frame\",\n              \"dangling_evidence_references\": 0,\n              \"canonical_tooling_limits\": [\n                  \"Standalone figure-validator entrypoint cannot import the absent parent src.orchestrator module; canonical JSON schema and strategy budgets checked locally.\",\n                  \"compile_latex.sh recorder checker rejects a system texmf.cnf symlink crossing two approved TeX roots. Sanitized pdflatex/BibTeX commands compile successfully; parent revalidation remains authoritative.\",\n                  \"System pdftotext is unavailable. Rendered SI checked from independent PyMuPDF extraction within the official text frame, excluding margin counters.\",\n                  \"Concept image is an orchestrator-owned placeholder until synchronous post-draft rendering.\"\n              ]}\n    (ROOT / \"local_validation_report.json\").write_text(json.dumps(report, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    print(json.dumps(report, indent=2))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
